            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions, and no configuration exists when \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad \n   D_s=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=s\\}\\;(2\\le s\\le n+1).\n   \\]\n   Thus \\(|D_s|=s-1\\) and \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).\n\n2. **Upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   *A sunny line meets each diagonal \\(D_s\\) in at most one point* (its slope is different from \\(-1\\)).  \n   A non‑sunny line is either horizontal, vertical or a diagonal of slope \\(-1\\).  \n   If \\(k\\ge n-1\\) then at most one line is non‑sunny.  \n   A non‑sunny diagonal can cover only one whole diagonal \\(D_s\\); the remaining diagonals must be covered by sunny lines, each of which contributes at most one point per diagonal.  \n   The two largest diagonals are \\(D_{n+1}\\) (size \\(n\\)) and \\(D_{n}\\) (size \\(n-1\\)).  \n   With a single non‑sunny line we can cover at most one of them completely; the other diagonal would then require at least its size distinct sunny lines, i.e. at least \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, which would already be all the sunny lines available.  Those \\(n-1\\) sunny lines would then have to cover the whole of \\(D_{n+1}\\) as well, which is impossible because a sunny line can give at most one point on \\(D_{n+1}\\).  Hence \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n3. **Construction for every \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n\n   *Step 1 – non‑sunny lines.*  \n   Take the \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_s:\\;x+y=s\\qquad (s=k+2,k+3,\\dots ,n+1).\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(\\ell_s\\) is non‑sunny and covers the whole diagonal \\(D_s\\).  Consequently all points of the diagonals \\(D_{k+2},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\) are already covered.\n\n   *Step 2 – sunny lines covering the remaining diagonals.*  \n   For \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y = i\\,x-(i-1).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(i\\neq0,-1,\\infty\\); therefore each \\(L_i\\) is sunny and the \\(L_i\\)’s are pairwise distinct.\n\n   Fix a diagonal \\(D_s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le k+1\\).  \n   Solving the system consisting of (2) and the equation of the diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   x=\\frac{s+i-1}{i+1},\\qquad \n   y=i\\,x-(i-1)=\\frac{i\\,s-i^{2}+1}{i+1}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) and \\(2\\le s\\le k+1\\), the numerator \\(s+i-1\\) is a multiple of the denominator \\(i+1\\); indeed  \n\n   \\[\n   s+i-1=(i+1)+(s-2)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad 0\\le s-2\\le k-1<i+1,\n   \\]\n\n   so the division yields the integer  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1+(s-2)\\frac{1}{i+1}\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,s-1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently \\((x,y)\\) given by (3) is a lattice point of the diagonal \\(D_s\\).  \n   Moreover, for a fixed \\(s\\) the points obtained from (3) when \\(i\\) runs from \\(1\\) to \\(k\\) are distinct, because the \\(x\\)-coordinates equal \\(s-i\\).  Hence each diagonal \\(D_s\\) contributes exactly one point to each line \\(L_i\\).\n\n   Therefore the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) together contain **all** points of the diagonals \\(D_2,\\dots ,D_{k+1}\\).\n\n   *Step 3 – counting.*  \n   The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_{k+2},\\ell_{k+3},\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\}\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\((n-k)+k=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  Hence a configuration with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines exists for every \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The impossibility proved in step 2 shows that \\(k\\ge n-1\\) cannot occur, while step 3 exhibits a concrete construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  Consequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
