ven \\(n\\) the case \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n3. **Construction for \\(k\\le n-1\\).**  \n   *Lemma 2.*  \n   For every \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exist\n   \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, covering \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Take the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k\n   \\]\n\n   (these are non‑sunny).  They cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose\n   abscissa does not exceed \\(n-k\\).  The remaining points form the set  \n\n   \\[\n   T'_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge n-k+1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   which is precisely the triangle \\(T_k\\) after the translation\n   \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-(n-k),b)\\).  Thus \\(|T'_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\).\n\n   We now cover \\(T'_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n   For \\(i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y = x + i .\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(L_i\\) has slope \\(1\\) (hence sunny) and meets \\(T'_k\\) in the\n   points \\(\\{(a,a+i)\\mid 1\\le a\\le \\lfloor\\frac{k+1-i}{2}\\rfloor\\}\\).\n   The families \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=0}^{k-1}\\) are pairwise distinct and together\n   contain every point of \\(T'_k\\) (a point \\((a,b)\\) of \\(T'_k\\) satisfies\n   \\(b-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\), so it lies on the unique line \\(L_{b-a}\\)).\n   Hence the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_0,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\) together with the\n   \\(n-k\\) vertical lines give a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines,\n   exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, covering all of \\(T_n\\). ∎  \n\n4. **Construction for \\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\) is odd.**  \n   Let \\(n=2m+1\\) (\\(m\\ge 1\\)).  Consider the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;y = x + c\\quad\\text{with }c\\in\\{-2m,-2m+2,\\dots ,2m\\}\\;}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the lines of slope \\(1\\) whose intercepts are the even integers\n   between \\(-n+1\\) and \\(n-1\\).  All these lines are sunny.  \n   For a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(b-a\\) an even integer between\n   \\(-n+1\\) and \\(n-1\\) (because \\(a+b\\) and \\(b-a\\) have the same parity).\n   Consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the unique line of (2) with\n   \\(c=b-a\\).  Hence the \\(n\\) sunny lines (2) cover the whole set\n   \\(T_n\\). ∎  \n\n5. **Putting everything together.**  \n   Lemma 2 gives the existence of configurations for every\n   \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  Lemma 1 shows that for even \\(n\\) the case\n   \\(k=n\\) cannot occur, while the construction in step 4 shows that for\n   odd \\(n\\) the case \\(k=n\\) does occur.  Therefore the list of admissible\n   values of \\(k\\) is exactly the one stated in the verdict.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\binom{n+1}{2}= \\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A bound for the number of points on a sunny line\n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nA sunny line meets \\(T_n\\) in at most  \n\n\\[\ns_n:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\npoints.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe convex hull of \\(T_n\\) is the right‑isosceles triangle\n\\(\\Delta_n=\\{(x,y)\\mid x\\ge 0,\\;y\\ge 0,\\;x+y\\le n+1\\}\\).\nA line that is not parallel to any side of \\(\\Delta_n\\) cuts the triangle\nin a segment whose two endpoints lie on two distinct sides.\nIf the line has slope \\(m\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\), the projection of the\nsegment on the \\(x\\)-axis has length at most \\(n+1\\) and contains at most\n\\(\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor+1\\) integer abscissae, because the step between\nconsecutive integer points on the line is at least \\(2\\) in the \\(x\\)-direction\n(the step is the denominator of the reduced fraction representing the slope).\nHence the number of lattice points of \\(T_n\\) on the line does not exceed\n\\(\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Consequences for the number of sunny lines\n\nLet a configuration contain \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\nA non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (a vertical line\n\\(x=i\\) contains exactly \\(n+1-i\\le n\\) points, and the same bound holds for\nhorizontal and diagonal lines).  Using Lemma 1 we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 =|T_n|\n   \\le k\\,s_n + (n-k)\\,n . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(k=n\\) then (1) becomes \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le n\\,s_n\\).\nBecause \\(s_n=\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\), the inequality holds only when\n\\(s_n=n/2\\), i.e. when \\(n\\) is odd.  Consequently\n\n* for **even** \\(n\\) the case \\(k=n\\) is impossible;\n* for **odd** \\(n\\) the inequality does not forbid \\(k=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Realising every \\(k\\le n-1\\)\n\n**Lemma 2.**  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there\nexists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, covering\n\\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThese are non‑sunny and together cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose first\ncoordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).  The uncovered points form\n\n\\[\nT'_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge n-k+1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe map \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-(n-k),b)\\) sends \\(T'_k\\) bijectively onto\n\\(T_k\\); therefore \\(|T'_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\).\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = x + i . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAll lines \\(L_i\\) have slope \\(1\\), hence are sunny, and they are pairwise\ndifferent.  A point \\((a,b)\\in T'_k\\) satisfies \\(b-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\);\nconsequently it lies on the unique line \\(L_{\\,b-a}\\).  Thus the \\(k\\) lines\n\\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=0}^{k-1}\\) together cover the whole set \\(T'_k\\).\n\nThe family consisting of the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (3) and the \\(k\\) sunny\nlines (4) contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, precisely \\(k\\) of which are\nsunny, and it covers every point of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The case \\(k=n\\) for odd \\(n\\)\n\nAssume \\(n=2m+1\\) (\\(m\\ge 1\\)).  Consider the \\(n\\) lines\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;y = x + c\\quad\\text{with }c\\in\\{-2m,-2m+2,\\dots ,2m\\}\\;}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nAll have slope \\(1\\), hence are sunny, and they are distinct.\nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\le 2m+2\\); therefore\n\\(b-a\\) is an even integer satisfying \\(-2m\\le b-a\\le 2m\\).\nConsequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the unique line of (5) whose intercept is\n\\(c=b-a\\).  Hence the \\(n\\) sunny lines (5) cover the whole set \\(T_n\\).\n\nThus for odd \\(n\\) a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Completion of the proof\n\n* From Lemma 2 we have a construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n* Lemma 1 shows that for even \\(n\\) the value \\(k=n\\) cannot occur.\n* For odd \\(n\\) the construction (5) provides a configuration with\n  \\(k=n\\).\n\nTherefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\ \\text{odd},\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\ \\text{even}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nThis completes the solution. ∎"
