 horizontal length is at most \\(n+1\\); consequently the number of lattice points on the segment is at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{q}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) because \\(q\\ge2\\) (the cases \\(q=0,1\\) correspond to slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), which are excluded). \u220e  \n\n3. **Global inequality.**  \n   A non\u2011sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (a vertical line \\(x=i\\) contains exactly \\(n+1-i\\le n\\) points, and the same bound holds for horizontal and diagonal lines).  \n   Hence, if a configuration uses \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, we have  \n   \\[\n   |T_n|\\le k\\,s_n+(n-k)\\,n .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) and \\(s_n=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) into (3) gives\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+(n-k)n .\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\(k\\) yields the universal bound\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-1 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Thus (1) holds for every admissible configuration.\n\n4. **Construction for any \\(k\\le n-1\\).**  \n   *Lemma\u202f2.* For every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, that covers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   - **Step\u202f1 (non\u2011sunny part).**  \n     Take the vertical lines\n     \\[\n     x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n     \\tag{5}\n     \\]\n     They are non\u2011sunny and together cover every point of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).\n\n   - **Step\u202f2 (the remaining points).**  \n     The points that are still uncovered are\n     \\[\n     T'_k=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge n-k+1\\}.\n     \\]\n     Write \\(a=n-k+u\\) with \\(u\\ge1\\); the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes \\(u+b\\le k+1\\).  Hence\n     \\[\n     T'_k=\\{(u,b)\\mid u\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;u+b\\le k+1\\},\n     \\]\n     i.e. a copy of \\(T_k\\) (the same triangular set with parameter \\(k\\)).\n\n   - **Step\u202f3 (sunny covering of a triangle).**  \n     For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(0\\le i\\le k-1\\) define the line\n     \\[\n     L_i:\\; y = x + i .\n     \\tag{6}\n     \\]\n     All these lines have slope \\(1\\), therefore they are sunny, and they are distinct because their intercepts differ.  \n     Let \\((u,b)\\in T_k\\).  Since \\(u+b\\le k+1\\), the difference \\(b-u\\) is an integer belonging to the set \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\).  Consequently \\((u,b)\\) lies on the unique line \\(L_{\\,b-u}\\).  Thus the family \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=0}^{k-1}\\) covers the whole set \\(T_k\\).\n\n   - **Step\u202f4 (assembling the configuration).**  \n     The \\(n\\) lines consisting of the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (5) together with the \\(k\\) sunny lines (6) are distinct, contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, and cover every point of \\(T_n\\). \u220e  \n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma\u202f2 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) is attainable.  \n   Inequality (4) shows that no configuration can have \\(k=n\\).  \n   Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n   The question whether a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines can exist for some \\(n\\) remains open; the present methods do not resolve it. \u220e"
