ferent from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Maximal number of points of \\(S_n\\) on a sunny line  \n\nConsider a line \\(L\\) whose slope is not \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nIf \\(L\\) contains two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in S_n\\) then,\nwriting the slope as a reduced fraction \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\;(p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\,\n\\gcd(p,q)=1,\\;q>0)\\), all points of \\(L\\cap\\mathbb Z^2\\) are of the form  \n\n\\[\n(a_1+mq,\\;b_1+mp),\\qquad m\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nHence the distance in the \\(x\\)-direction between consecutive lattice points\non \\(L\\) equals \\(q\\ge 1\\).  Condition \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) gives  \n\n\\[\na+b\\le n+1\\Longrightarrow\na_1+b_1+ m(p+q)\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(p+q\\ge 1\\), the number of admissible integers \\(m\\) is at most\n\\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-(a_1+b_1)}{p+q}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\n\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\nConsequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Every sunny line meets at most }\nM_n:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\text{ points of }S_n.}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe bound is attained by the line \\(y=x\\), which contains the points\n\\((1,1),(2,2),\\dots ,\\bigl(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor,\n\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\bigr)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A necessary inequality for the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of\n\\(S_n\\).  Let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines; the remaining\n\\(n-k\\) lines are vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\).  \nA non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (e.g. the line\n\\(x=1\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) points).  Counting incidences we obtain\n\n\\[\n(n-k)\\,n\\;+\\;k\\,M_n\\;\\ge\\;N_n .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(M_n\\) from (1) and \\(N_n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\); after elementary\nmanipulation we get\n\n\\[\n2k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac n2