ints of \\(T_k\\); together they\ncover \\(M_k^2\\) points.\n\nIf after using the lines (8) some points of \\(T_k\\) are still uncovered,\nassign each of those points to a distinct remaining sunny line\n(e.g. the line through the point with slope \\(2\\); any slope different from\n\\(0,\\infty,-1\\) makes the line sunny).  Because of (7) the number of\nremaining points does not exceed the number of remaining sunny lines, so\nthis can always be done.\n\nThus we obtain \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines whose union contains \\(T_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.4  The whole configuration  \n\nThe family consisting of the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (5) together with the\n\\(k\\) sunny lines constructed in §3.3 is a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines,\nexactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least\none of them.  Hence for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (4) such a\nconfiguration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Completion of the proof  \n\nWe have proved:\n\n*If a configuration of \\(n\\) lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) contains\n\\(k\\) sunny lines, then \\(k\\) must satisfy (4).*\n\n*Conversely, for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (4) we have exhibited a\nconfiguration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.*\n\nTherefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely the\nset described in (4).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n\\ge 3,\\;\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\text{ odd},\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\text{ even}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]"
