annot be realised.  Consequently any admissible \\(k>0\\) must be\n   odd.\n\n4. **Sufficiency.**  \n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy the conditions obtained in (3) and be odd (or\n   \\(k=0\\)).  \n\n   *Non‑sunny lines.*  \n   Choose \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   They are distinct, non‑sunny and cover every point of \\(S_n\\) whose\n   first coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).\n\n   *Remaining points.*  \n   After deleting the points covered by (5) the still uncovered set is  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge 1,\\ a+b\\le k+1\\}=S_k .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   *Sunny lines covering \\(S_k\\).*  \n   Because \\(k\\) is odd, write \\(k=2t+1\\).  Then\n   \\(M_k=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=t+1\\) and  \n\n   \\[\n   kM_k=(2t+1)(t+1)=\\frac{(2t+1)(2t+2)}2=|S_k|.\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the \\(k\\) sunny lines can be chosen so that each of them contains\n   exactly \\(M_k\\) points of \\(S_k\\) and together they cover all of \\(S_k\\).\n   An explicit construction is:\n\n   * for \\(j=0,1,\\dots ,t\\) take the line \\(y=x+j\\); it passes through the\n     points \\((1+j,1), (2+j,2),\\dots ,(t+1+j,t+1)\\) of \\(S_k\\) and thus\n     contains \\(M_k=t+1\\) points;\n   * the remaining \\(t\\) sunny lines (if any) are taken through the still\n     uncovered points, each line having a slope different from\n     \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) (possible because a single lattice point can be placed\n     on a line of any admissible slope).\n\n   The lines in (5) together with the \\(k\\) sunny lines constructed above\n   form a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny,\n   covering every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary restrictions (3) together with the parity argument (4)\n   give the set of admissible \\(k\\).  The explicit construction shows that\n   every integer in that set can indeed be realised.  Hence the description\n   in the boxed answer is complete.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Points of \\(S_m\\) on a sunny line  \n\nConsider a line \\(L\\) whose slope is not \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nWrite its slope as a reduced fraction \\(\\dfrac{p}{q}\\) with\n\\(p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;q>0,\\;\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) and \\(p\\neq 0,\\;p\\neq -q\\).\nIf \\((x_0,y_0)\\in L\\cap\\mathbb Z^2\\) then all integer points of \\(L\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(x_0+mq,\\;y_0+mp),\\qquad m\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus the difference in the \\(x\\)-coordinate between consecutive lattice\npoints on \\(L\\) equals \\(q\\ge 1\\).  For a point \\((x,y)\\in S_m\\) we have\n\\(x+y\\le m+1\\).  If \\((x_0,y_0)\\) is the first lattice point of \\(L\\) that\nlies in \\(S_m\\), then the largest admissible integer \\(m\\) in (1) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nx_0+mq+y_0+mp\\le m+1\\Longrightarrow\nm\\le\\frac{m+1-(x_0+y_0)}{p+q}\\le\\frac{m}{2}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently the number of lattice points of \\(L\\) belonging to \\(S_m\\) is at\nmost  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)) actually attains this bound, because it\ncontains the points \\((1,1),(2,2),\\dots ,\\bigl(\\lfloor\\frac{m+1}{2}\\rfloor,\n\\lfloor\\frac{m+1}{2}\\rfloor\\bigr)\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{ \\text{Any sunny line meets at most }M_m:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\text{ points of }S_m.}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A necessary inequality for the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and that\nexactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  The remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are vertical,\nhorizontal or of slope \\(-1\\); each such line can contain at most \\(n\\) points\nof \\(S_n\\) (the line \\(x=1\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) points, and any other\nnon‑sunny line contains fewer).  Counting incidences gives  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)n+kM_n\\ge |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(M_n=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) from (2) and simplify.\nIf \\(n=2t\\) is even, then \\(M_n=t\\) and (3) becomes  \n\n\\[\n2t^2-kt\\ge 2t^2+t\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;k\\le 2t-1=n-1 .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(n=2t+1\\) is odd, then \\(M_n=t+1\\) and (3) yields  \n\n\\[\n(2t+1)^2-(2t+1)k\\ge (2t+1)(t+1)\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;k\\le 2t+1=n .\n\\]\n\nThus\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn-1, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[1mm]\nn,   & n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Parity restriction  \n\nLet \\(k>0\\) be even, say \\(k=2t\\).  After removing the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines\n(as described in the construction below) the still uncovered points form a\ncopy of \\(S_k\\).  By (2) each sunny line can meet at most  \n\n\\[\nM_k=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{2t+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor=t\n\\]\n\npoints of this set.  Hence the total number of points that the \\(k\\) sunny\nlines can cover is at most \\(kM_k=2t\\cdot t=t(2t)=k^2/2\\), whereas  \n\n\\[\n|S_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2=\\frac{2t(2t+1)}2=t(2t+1)=\\frac{k^2}{2}+\\frac{k}{2}>kM_k .\n\\]\n\nTherefore an even positive \\(k\\) cannot be realised.  Consequently any\nadmissible \\(k>0\\) must be **odd**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction for all admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy the conditions obtained in Sections 2–3, i.e.\n\n* \\(k=0\\), or\n* \\(k\\) is odd and \\(k\\le n\\) (if \\(n\\) odd) or \\(k\\le n-1\\) (if \\(n\\) even).\n\nWe exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, covering all\npoints of \\(S_n\\).\n\n#### 4.1  Non‑sunny lines  \n\nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThese are distinct, non‑sunny and together cover every point of \\(S_n\\) whose\nfirst coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).\n\n#### 4.2  The uncovered set  \n\nAfter deleting the points covered by (5) the remaining points are  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge 1,\\ a+b\\le k+1\\}=S_k .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus we have reduced the problem to covering the triangle \\(S_k\\) with exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 4.3  Covering \\(S_k\\) when \\(k\\) is odd  \n\nWrite \\(k=2t+1\\) (\\(t\\ge 0\\)).  Then, by (2),\n\n\\[\nM_k=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor=t+1,\n\\qquad\nkM_k=(2t+1)(t+1)=\\frac{(2t+1)(2t+2)}2=|S_k|.\n\\]\n\nHence it is possible to partition \\(S_k\\) into \\(k\\) subsets, each of size\n\\(M_k\\), and to place a sunny line through each subset.  An explicit\nrealisation is:\n\n* for \\(j=0,1,\\dots ,t\\) take the line  \n\n  \\[\n  \\ell_j:\\;y=x+j .\n  \\tag{7}\n  \\]\n\n  The line \\(\\ell_j\\) contains the points  \n\n  \\[\n  (1+j,1),\\;(2+j,2),\\dots ,(t+1+j,t+1),\n  \\]\n\n  i.e. exactly \\(M_k=t+1\\) points of \\(S_k\\).\n\n* The remaining \\(t\\) sunny lines (if any) are taken through the still\n  uncovered points; each such line can be given any slope different from\n  \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so it is sunny.\n\nAll lines in (7) have slope \\(1\\), which is admissible (it is not \\(0\\),\n\\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\)).  The lines are distinct because they have different\n\\(y\\)-intercepts.  Consequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover every\npoint of \\(S_k\\).\n\n#### 4.4  Putting everything together  \n\nThe family consisting of the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (5) and the \\(k\\) sunny\nlines constructed in §4.3 is a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of\nthem sunny, and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Completion of the proof  \n\n* From (4) and the parity argument we have shown that any feasible \\(k\\)\n  must satisfy  \n\n  \\[\n  k=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\n  k\\text{ odd},\\;\n  k\\le\n  \\begin{cases}\n  n,   & n\\text{ odd},\\\\\n  n-1, & n\\text{ even}.\n  \\end{cases}\n  \\tag{8}\n  \\]\n\n* The construction of Section 4 proves that every integer \\(k\\) obeying (8)\n  can indeed be realised.\n\nTherefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly the set\ndescribed in (8), which we rewrite as\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,3,5,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\ \\text{odd},\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,3,5,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\ \\text{even}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]"
