\\(S_n\\).\n\n#### 4.1  Non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThese are distinct, non\u2011sunny and together cover every point of \\(S_n\\) whose\nfirst coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).\n\n#### 4.2  The uncovered set  \n\nAfter deleting the points covered by (5) the remaining points are  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge 1,\\ a+b\\le k+1\\}=S_k .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus we have reduced the problem to covering the triangle \\(S_k\\) with exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 4.3  Covering \\(S_k\\) when \\(k\\) is odd  \n\nWrite \\(k=2t+1\\) (\\(t\\ge 0\\)).  Then, by (2),\n\n\\[\nM_k=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor=t+1,\n\\qquad\nkM_k=(2t+1)(t+1)=\\frac{(2t+1)(2t+2)}2=|S_k|.\n\\]\n\nHence it is possible to partition \\(S_k\\) into \\(k\\) subsets, each of size\n\\(M_k\\), and to place a sunny line through each subset.  An explicit\nrealisation is:\n\n* for \\(j=0,1,\\dots ,t\\) take the line  \n\n  \\[\n  \\ell_j:\\;y=x+j .\n  \\tag{7}\n  \\]\n\n  The line \\(\\ell_j\\) contains the points  \n\n  \\[\n  (1+j,1),\\;(2+j,2),\\dots ,(t+1+j,t+1),\n  \\]\n\n  i.e. exactly \\(M_k=t+1\\) points of \\(S_k\\).\n\n* The remaining \\(t\\) sunny lines (if any) are taken through the still\n  uncovered points; each such line can be given any slope different from\n  \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so it is sunny.\n\nAll lines in (7) have slope \\(1\\), which is admissible (it is not \\(0\\),\n\\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\)).  The lines are distinct because they have different\n\\(y\\)-intercepts.  Consequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover every\npoint of \\(S_k\\).\n\n#### 4.4  Putting everything together  \n\nThe family consisting of the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (5) and the \\(k\\) sunny\nlines constructed in \u00a74.3 is a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of\nthem sunny, and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n---------------------------------------------