s complete.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Points of \\(S_m\\) on a sunny line  \n\nConsider a line \\(L\\) whose slope is not \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nWrite its slope as a reduced fraction \\(\\dfrac{p}{q}\\) with\n\\(p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;q>0,\\;\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) and \\(p\\neq 0,\\;p\\neq -q\\).\nIf \\((x_0,y_0)\\in L\\cap\\mathbb Z^2\\) then all integer points of \\(L\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(x_0+mq,\\;y_0+mp),\\qquad m\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus the difference in the \\(x\\)-coordinate between consecutive lattice\npoints on \\(L\\) equals \\(q\\ge 1\\).  For a point \\((x,y)\\in S_m\\) we have\n\\(x+y\\le m+1\\).  If \\((x_0,y_0)\\) is the first lattice point of \\(L\\) that\nlies in \\(S_m\\), then the largest admissible integer \\(m\\) in (1) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nx_0+mq+y_0+mp\\le m+1\\Longrightarrow\nm\\le\\frac{m+1-(x_0+y_0)}{p+q}\\le\\frac{m}{2}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently the number of lattice points of \\(L\\) belonging to \\(S_m\\) is at\nmost  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)) actually attains this bound, because it\ncontains the points \\((1,1),(2,2),\\dots ,\\bigl(\\lfloor\\frac{m+1}{2}\\rfloor,\n\\lfloor\\frac{m+1}{2}\\rfloor\\bigr)\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{ \\text{Any sunny line meets at most }M_m:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\text{ points of }S_m.}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A necessary inequality for the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and that\nexactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  The remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are vertical,\nhorizontal or of slope \\(-1\\); each such line can contain at most \\(n\\) points\nof \\(S_n\\) (the line \\(x=1\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) points, and any other\nnon‑sunny line contains fewer).  Counting incidences gives  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)n+kM_n\\ge |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(M_n=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) from (2) and simplify.\nIf \\(n=2t\\) is even, then \\(M_n=t\\) and (3) becomes  \n\n\\[\n2t^2-kt\\ge 2t^2+t\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;k\\le 2t-1=n-1 .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(n=2t+1\\) is odd, then \\(M_n=t+1\\) and (3) yields  \n\n\\[\n(2t+1)^2-(2t+1)k\\ge (2t+1)(t+1)\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;k\\le 2t+1=n .\n\\]\n\nThus\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn-1, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[1mm]\nn,   & n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Parity restriction  \n\nLet \\(k>0\\) be even, say \\(k=2t\\).  After removing the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines\n(as described in the construction below) the still uncovered points form a\ncopy of \\(S_k\\).  By (2) each sunny line can meet at most  \n\n\\[\nM_k=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{2t+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor=t\n\\]\n\npoints of this set.  Hence the total number of points that the \\(k\\) sunny\nlines can cover is at most \\(kM_k=2t\\cdot t=t(2t)=k^2/2\\), whereas  \n\n\\[\n|S_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2=\\frac{2t(2t+1)}2=t(2t+1)=\\frac{k^2}{2}+\\frac{k}{2}>kM_k .\n\\]\n\nTherefore an even positive \\(k\\) cannot be realised.  Consequently any\nadmissible \\(k>0\\) must be **odd**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction for all admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy the conditions obtained in Sections 2–3, i.e.\n\n* \\(k=0\\), or\n* \\(k\\) is odd and \\(k\\le n\\) (if \\(n\\) odd) or \\(k\\le n-1\\) (if \\(n\\) even).\n\nWe exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, covering all\npoints of \\(S_n\\).\n