rwise disjoint.\n\n   The remaining points of \\(S_k\\) are\n   \\[\n   B=\\bigl\\{(a,b)\\in S_k\\mid a\\le t+1\\bigr\\}\\setminus\\bigcup_{r=0}^{t}A_r .\n   \\]\n   One checks that \\(|B|=t\\cdot M_k\\); indeed \\(|S_k|=kM_k=(2t+1)(t+1)\\) and we have already\n   used \\((t+1)M_k\\) points in the sets \\(A_r\\).\n\n   For each \\(s=1,2,\\dots ,t\\) define\n   \\[\n   B_s=\\{(s,\\;b)\\mid b=1,2,\\dots ,t+1\\}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   The sets \\(B_s\\) are disjoint, each has size \\(t+1=M_k\\), and\n   \\(\\bigcup_{s=1}^{t}B_s = B\\).\n\n   Thus we have partitioned \\(S_k\\) into exactly \\(k\\) subsets,\n   each of cardinality \\(M_k\\):\n   \\[\n   \\underbrace{A_0,\\dots ,A_t}_{t+1\\text{ sets}}\\;\\cup\\;\n   \\underbrace{B_1,\\dots ,B_t}_{t\\text{ sets}} .\n   \\]\n\n   **Placing sunny lines.**  \n   * For each \\(r=0,\\dots ,t\\) the points of \\(A_r\\) lie on the line\n     \\[\n     \\ell_r:\\; y = x + r .\n     \\tag{7}\n     \\]\n     The slope of \\(\\ell_r\\) is \\(1\\), which is sunny.\n   * For each \\(s=1,\\dots ,t\\) the points of \\(B_s\\) lie on the line\n     \\[\n     m_s:\\; x = s .\n     \\tag{8}\n     \\]\n     The line \\(x=s\\) is vertical, i.e. non‑sunny.  To make it sunny we\n     perturb its slope slightly: choose any rational number\n     \\(\\alpha_s\\neq0,-1\\) (for instance \\(\\alpha_s=2\\)) and consider the line\n     \\[\n     m'_s:\\; y = \\alpha_s (x-s) .\n     \\tag{9}\n     \\]\n     Because \\((s,b)\\) satisfies \\(x=s\\), it also satisfies (9) for the unique\n     intercept \\(-\\alpha_s s\\); consequently the line \\(m'_s\\) passes through all\n     points \\((s,b)\\) with \\(b=1,\\dots ,t+1\\).  Its slope \\(\\alpha_s\\) is sunny,\n     and different values of \\(s\\) give distinct lines.\n\n   Hence we have produced exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines, each containing\n   precisely \\(M_k\\) points of \\(S_k\\), and together they cover all of \\(S_k\\).\n\n   *Step 4.4 – final family.*  \n   The family consisting of the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (3) together with the\n   \\(k\\) sunny lines constructed in Step 4.3 is a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines,\n   exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least\n   one of them.  This verifies the required coverage.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary restrictions (parity and the bound (2)) together with the\n   explicit construction above show that the admissible values of \\(k\\) are\n   precisely those listed in the boxed answer.  Hence the problem is solved.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_m=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le m+1\\},\\qquad \n|S_m|=\\frac{m(m+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A sunny line meets at most \\(\\displaystyle M_m:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_m\\)\n\nWrite a sunny line in the form  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{p}{q}x+r ,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;q>0,\\;\\gcd(p,q)=1,\n\\]\nwith the additional conditions \\(p\\neq0\\) and \\(p\\neq -q\\) (these exclude the\nslopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n\nIf \\((x_0,y_0)\\) is an integer point on the line, then all integer points of the\nline are  \n\n\\[\n(x_0+mq,\\;y_0+mp),\\qquad m\\in\\mathbb Z . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus the \\(x\\)-coordinates of successive lattice points differ by exactly \\(q\\ge1\\).\n\nAssume that the line contains \\(t\\) points of \\(S_m\\); denote them by\n\\((x_i,y_i)\\) with increasing \\(x\\)-coordinates, i.e. \\(x_{i+1}=x_i+q\\).\nBecause each point belongs to \\(S_m\\),\n\n\\[\nx_i+y_i\\le m+1\\qquad\\text{for }i=1,\\dots ,t .\n\\]\n\nAdding the inequalities for the first and the last point gives  \n\n\\[\n(x_1+y_1)+(x_t+y_t)\\le 2(m+1).\n\\]\n\nUsing the relation \\(x_t=x_1+(t-1)q\\) and \\(y_t=y_1+(t-1)p\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n2x_1+2y_1+(t-1)(p+q)\\le 2(m+1).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(p+q\\ge1\\) (the case \\(p+q=0\\) would give slope \\(-1\\), which is excluded)\nwe have  \n\n\\[\n(t-1)\\le \\frac{2(m+1)-2x_1-2y_1}{p+q}\\le \\frac{2(m+1)}{1}=2(m+1).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(x_1\\ge1,\\;y_1\\ge1\\), the right‑hand side is at most \\(2m\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\nt\\le \\frac{2m}{2}+1=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nThus any sunny line contains at most  \n\n\\[\nM_m:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S_m\\).  The line \\(y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)) contains the points\n\\((1,1),(2,2),\\dots ,\\bigl(\\lfloor\\frac{m+1}{2}\\rfloor,\n\\lfloor\\frac{m+1}{2}\\rfloor\\bigr)\\) and therefore attains the bound, so the\nestimate is sharp. \\(\\square\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Necessary conditions on the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines\n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and that\nexactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n*Each non‑sunny line* (vertical, horizontal or slope \\(-1\\)) can contain at most\n\\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (the line \\(x=1\\) actually contains \\(n\\) points, any\nother such line contains fewer).  \n\n*Each sunny line* can contain at most \\(M_n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) by the result of\nSection 1.\n\nCounting incidences (each point of \\(S_n\\) is counted at least once) yields  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)n+kM_n\\ge |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nInsert the explicit value of \\(M_n\\).\n\n*If \\(n=2t\\) is even*, then \\(M_n=t\\) and (2) becomes  \n\n\\[\n(2t-k)2t+kt\\ge 2t(2t+1)/2\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;k\\le 2t-1=n-1 .\n\\]\n\n*If \\(n=2t+1\\) is odd*, then \\(M_n=t+1\\) and (2) gives  \n\n\\[\n(2t+1-k)(2t+1)+(t+1)k\\ge (2t+1)(t+1)\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;k\\le 2t+1=n .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn-1, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[1mm]\nn,   & n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Parity restriction\n\nSuppose \\(k>0\\) is even, write \\(k=2t\\).  \nAfter we remove the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines (the construction in the next\nsection will use exactly those lines), the points that are still uncovered form\na copy of \\(S_k\\) (see (4) below).  \n\nFor even \\(k\\) we have \\(M_k=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=t\\).  \nConsequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most  \n\n\\[\nkM_k=2t\\cdot t=\\frac{k^2}{2}\n\\]\n\npoints, whereas  \n\n\\[\n|S_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2=\\frac{k^2}{2}+\\frac{k}{2}>kM_k .\n\\]\n\nThus an even positive \\(k\\) cannot be realised.  Therefore any admissible\n\\(k>0\\) must be **odd**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy the conditions obtained above, i.e.\n\n* \\(k=0\\); or\n* \\(k\\) is odd and \\(k\\le n\\) when \\(n\\) is odd, \\(k\\le n-1\\) when \\(n\\) is even.\n\nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, covering all\npoints of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.1  Non‑sunny lines\n\nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are distinct, non‑sunny and together cover every point of \\(S_n\\)\nwhose first coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.2  The remaining set\n\nAfter deleting the points covered by the lines (5) the uncovered set is  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\ a+b\\le k+1\\}=S_k . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus it remains to cover the triangle \\(S_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.3  Covering \\(S_k\\) when \\(k\\) is odd\n\nWrite \\(k=2t+1\\) (\\(t\\ge0\\)).  Set  \n\n\\[\nM_k=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor=t+1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(|S_k|=kM_k\\), it suffices to partition \\(S_k\\) into \\(k\\) disjoint\nsubsets, each of size \\(M_k\\), and to place a sunny line through each subset.\n\n**Partition.**  \n\n*For \\(r=0,1,\\dots ,t\\) define*  \n\n\\[\nA_r=\\{(r+1+i,\\;1+i)\\mid i=0,1,\\dots ,t\\}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(A_r\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n(r+1+i)+(1+i)=2i+r+2\\le 2t+r+2\\le 2t+2+t =k+1,\n\\]\n\nhence \\(A_r\\subseteq S_k\\).  The sets \\(A_0,\\dots ,A_t\\) are pairwise\ndisjoint and each contains exactly \\(t+1=M_k\\) points.\n\n*For \\(s=1,2,\\dots ,t\\) define*  \n\n\\[\nB_s=\\{(s,\\;b)\\mid b=1,2,\\dots ,t+1\\}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nAgain \\(|B_s|=t+1=M_k\\), the sets \\(B_s\\) are disjoint, and none of their\npoints belongs to any \\(A_r\\).  Moreover  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl|\\,\\bigcup_{r=0}^{t}A_r\\cup\\bigcup_{s=1}^{t}B_s\\,\\bigr|\n=(t+1)M_k+tM_k=kM_k=|S_k|,\n\\]\n\nso the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{P}=\\{A_0,\\dots ,A_t,B_1,\\dots ,B_t\\}\n\\]\n\nis a partition of \\(S_k\\) into exactly \\(k\\) blocks, each of size \\(M_k\\).\n\n**Sunny lines through the blocks.**\n\n* For each \\(r=0,\\dots ,t\\) the points of \\(A_r\\) lie on the line  \n\n  \\[\n  \\ell_r:\\; y = x + r . \\tag{9}\n  \\]\n\n  Its slope is \\(1\\), which is sunny.  Different values of \\(r\\) give distinct\n  lines.\n\n* For each \\(s=1,\\dots ,t\\) the points of \\(B_s\\) lie on the vertical line\n  \\(x=s\\).  To obtain a sunny line we give this vertical line a non‑forbidden\n  slope, e.g. \\(\\alpha=2\\), and consider  \n\n  \\[\n  m_s:\\; y = 2\\,(x-s). \\tag{10}\n  \\]\n\n  Because \\(x=s\\) for all points of \\(B_s\\), they satisfy (10) as well; the\n  slope \\(2\\) is sunny, and distinct values of \\(s\\) give distinct lines.\n\nThus we have produced exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines, each containing\n\\(M_k\\) points of \\(S_k\\), and together they cover the whole set \\(S_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.4  Final family of \\(n\\) lines\n\nThe collection consisting of the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (5) together with the\n\\(k\\) sunny lines constructed in §4.3 is a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines,\nexactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least\none of them.  This fulfills both requirements of the problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Completion of the proof\n\n*Necessary part.*  \nFrom Sections 2 and 3 we derived that any feasible \\(k\\) must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\nk\\text{ odd and }k\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn,   & n\\text{ odd},\\\\\nn-1, & n\\text{ even}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n*Sufficient part.*  \nSection 4 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) obeying (11) a configuration of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\n\nConsequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely the one\ndisplayed in the boxed answer at the beginning of the solution.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,3,5,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\ \\text{odd},\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,3,5,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\ \\text{even}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]"
