-(x_0+y_0)}{p+q}\\le\\frac{m}{p+q}.\n   \\]\n   Since \\(p+q\\ge2\\) (both \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) are non‑zero and have the same sign,\n   otherwise the slope would be \\(-1\\)), we obtain\n   \\[\n   t\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   *Case 2 – \\(p+q<0\\).*  \n   Now the sum \\(x+y\\) decreases when \\(t\\) grows.  The lower bound\n   \\(x+y\\ge2\\) (because \\(x,y\\ge1\\)) yields\n   \\[\n   t\\le\\frac{x_0+y_0-2}{|p+q|}\\le\\frac{m}{|p+q|}\n   \\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   In both cases the number of admissible integer values of \\(t\\) is at most\n   \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1=\n   \\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  Hence\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\text{Any sunny line contains at most }M_m:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\text{ points of }S_m.}\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   The bound is sharp: the line \\(y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)) meets exactly\n   \\(M_m\\) points of \\(S_m\\).\n\n3. **Necessary conditions on the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.**  \n\n   *Incidence count.*  \n   The \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines can each contain at most \\(n\\) points of\n   \\(S_n\\); each sunny line can contain at most \\(M_n\\) points by (3).  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-k)n+kM_n\\ge |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Solving (4) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n-1, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[1mm]\n   n,   & n\\text{ odd}.\n   \\end{cases}\\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   *Parity.*  \n   Suppose \\(k>0\\) is even, say \\(k=2t\\).  \n   After removing the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines (see the construction below) the\n   still uncovered points form a copy of \\(S_k\\).  For even \\(k\\) we have\n   \\(M_k=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=t\\).  Consequently the \\(k\\)\n   sunny lines can cover at most \\(kM_k=2t\\cdot t=\\frac{k^{2}}2\\) points,\n   whereas \\(|S_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2=\\frac{k^{2}}2+\\frac{k}{2}>kM_k\\).  \n   Thus an even positive \\(k\\) cannot be realised.  Therefore any admissible\n   \\(k>0\\) must be **odd**.\n\n   Combining (5) with the parity restriction yields the set of candidates\n   displayed in the boxed answer.\n\n4. **Sufficiency – explicit construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy the conditions above, i.e.\n\n   * \\(k=0\\); or\n   * \\(k\\) is odd and \\(k\\le n\\) when \\(n\\) is odd, \\(k\\le n-1\\) when \\(n\\) is even.\n\n   We shall build \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, covering all\n   points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   --------------------------------------------------------------------\n   **4.1  Non‑sunny lines.**  \n   Take the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k). \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   They are distinct, non‑sunny and together cover every point of \\(S_n\\) whose\n   first coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).\n\n   --------------------------------------------------------------------\n   **4.2  The remaining set.**  \n   After deleting the points covered by the lines (6) the uncovered set is  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\ a+b\\le k+1\\}=S_k . \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence it remains to cover the triangle \\(S_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny\n   lines.\n\n   --------------------------------------------------------------------\n   **4.3  Covering \\(S_k\\) when \\(k\\) is odd.**  \n\n   Write \\(k=2t+1\\) (\\(t\\ge0\\)).  Set  \n\n   \\[\n   M_k=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor=t+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(|S_k|=kM_k\\), it suffices to partition \\(S_k\\) into \\(k\\) disjoint\n   subsets, each of size \\(M_k\\), and to place a sunny line through each subset.\n\n   **Partition.**  \n   For each integer \\(r\\) with \\(-t\\le r\\le t\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   D_r=\\{(a,b)\\in S_k\\mid b-a=r\\}. \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   The condition \\(b-a=r\\) together with \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) forces  \n\n   \\[\n   a\\in\\Bigl\\{\\,\\max\\{1,1+r\\},\\; \\max\\{1,1+r\\}+1,\\dots ,\n   \\; \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1+r}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   A short computation shows that for every \\(r\\) with \\(-t\\le r\\le t\\) the\n   set \\(D_r\\) contains exactly \\(t+1=M_k\\) points.  Moreover the sets\n   \\(\\{D_r\\}_{r=-t}^{t}\\) are pairwise disjoint and their union is the whole\n   \\(S_k\\).  Thus we have obtained a partition of \\(S_k\\) into exactly\n   \\(k=2t+1\\) blocks, each of cardinality \\(M_k\\).\n\n   **Sunny lines.**  \n   All points of a block \\(D_r\\) lie on the line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_r:\\; y = x + r . \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(\\ell_r\\) is \\(1\\), which is sunny (it is different from\n   \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  Distinct values of \\(r\\) give distinct lines, so we have\n   produced exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(S_k\\).\n\n   --------------------------------------------------------------------\n   **4.4  Final family of \\(n\\) lines.**  \n\n   The family consisting of the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (6) together with the\n   \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_r\\) (\\(r=-t,\\dots ,t\\)) is a set of \\(n\\) distinct\n   lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at\n   least one of them.  This verifies both requirements of the problem.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary restrictions (parity and the bound (5)) together with the\n   explicit construction above show that the admissible values of \\(k\\) are\n   precisely those listed in the boxed answer.  Hence the problem is solved. \\(\\square\\)\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
