nd is sharp: the line \\(y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)) meets exactly \\(M_m\\) points\n   of \\(S_m\\).\n\n3. **Necessary conditions on the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.**  \n\n   *Incidence count.*  \n   The \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines can each contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\)\n   (they are vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   Using (4) for the sunny lines we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-k)n+kM_n\\ge |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(M_n=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\), solving (5) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n-1, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[1mm]\n   n,   & n\\text{ odd}.\n   \\end{cases}\\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   *Parity.*  \n   Assume \\(k>0\\) is even, say \\(k=2t\\).  \n   After removing the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines (see the construction below) the\n   uncovered set is exactly \\(S_k\\).  \n   For even \\(k\\) we have \\(M_k=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=t\\).  \n   Hence the \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(kM_k=2t\\cdot t=\\frac{k^{2}}2\\) points,\n   whereas \\(|S_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2=\\frac{k^{2}}2+\\frac{k}{2}>kM_k\\).  \n   Thus an even positive \\(k\\) is impossible.  Consequently every admissible\n   \\(k>0\\) must be **odd**.\n\n   Combining the parity restriction with (6) yields the list of candidates\n   displayed in the boxed answer.\n\n4. **Sufficiency – explicit construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy the conditions above, i.e.\n\n   * \\(k=0\\); or\n   * \\(k\\) is odd and \\(k\\le n\\) when \\(n\\) is odd, \\(k\\le n-1\\) when \\(n\\) is even.\n\n   We shall build \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, covering all\n   points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   --------------------------------------------------------------------\n   **4.1  Non‑sunny lines.**  \n   Take the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k). \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   They are distinct, non‑sunny and together cover every point of \\(S_n\