1}\\,x+\\frac{t+1+r}{t+1},\n   \\qquad r=0,1,\\dots ,2t .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   *Each \\(\\ell_r\\) is sunny*: its slope \\(-\\dfrac{t}{t+1}\\) is different from\n   \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n\n   *Integer points on \\(\\ell_r\\).*  \n   Substituting \\(x=1+r\\) gives \\(y=1\\); thus \\((1+r,1)\\in\\ell_r\\cap S_k\\).\n   Adding the integer direction vector \\((t+1,-t)\\) repeatedly we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   (x_m,y_m)=\\bigl(1+r+m(t+1),\\;1-mt\\bigr),\\qquad m=0,1,\\dots ,t .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   For these values we have \\(x_m\\ge1,\\;y_m\\ge1\\) and  \n\n   \\[\n   x_m+y_m = r+2+m\\le 2t+2=k+1 ,\n   \\]\n\n   so every \\((x_m,y_m)\\) lies in \\(S_k\\).  \n   Conversely, any point of \\(S_k\\) satisfying \\(x-y=r\\) is of the form\n   (3) for a unique \\(m\\).  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_r\\cap S_k=\\{(x_m,y_m)\\mid m=0,1,\\dots ,t\\},\n   \\]\n\n   which contains exactly \\(t+1=\\frac{k+1}{2}\\) points.  \n\n   The sets \\(\\ell_r\\cap S_k\\) for different \\(r\\) are disjoint because they\n   correspond to different values of \\(x-y\\).  Their union is precisely\n   \\(S_k\\) (every point of \\(S_k\\) has a unique value of \\(x-y\\) between\n   \\(-t\\) and \\(t\\)).  Thus the \\(k=2t+1\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_0,\\dots ,\n   \\ell_{2t}\\) cover all points of \\(S_k\\).\n\n5. **Putting everything together.**  \n\n   * Choose the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) (non‑sunny).  \n   * Choose the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_0,\\dots ,\\ell_{k-1}\\) described in\n     (2).  \n\n   The two families are disjoint, there are exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, and\n   every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the\n   lines (the first family covers the points with \\(a\\le n-k\\); the second\n   family covers the remaining points, which form the set \\(S_k\\)).  \n\n   The construction works for every odd \\(k\\) satisfying the bound (1), and\n   for \\(k=0\\) we simply take the \\(n\\) vertical lines.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary conditions (parity and bound (1)) are also sufficient by the\n   explicit construction.  Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n   exactly the one displayed in the boxed answer.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,3,5,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\text{ odd},\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,3,5,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\text{ even}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\] \\(\\square\\)\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
