ny, and each point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\)\n  lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  Hence a configuration with exactly \\(0\\) sunny\n  lines exists.\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Take the sunny line \\(L_1:\\;y=x\\).  It contains the points\n  \\((i,i)\\) for all admissible \\(i\\).  The remaining points of \\(T_n\\) are\n  covered by the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\).  Thus we obtain a\n  configuration with exactly one sunny line.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Non\u2011existence for \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there is a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) lines\ncovering \\(T_n\\) with exactly two sunny lines, say \\(S_1,S_2\\).  Let\n\\(\\mathcal B=\\mathcal F\\setminus\\{S_1,S_2\\}\\); then \\(|\\mathcal B|=n-2\\) and every\nline in \\(\\mathcal B\\) is bad, i.e. it is either vertical, horizontal or has slope\n\\(-1\\).\n\nA vertical bad line fixes an \\(x\\)-coordinate, a horizontal bad line fixes a\n\\(y\\)-coordinate, and a line of slope \\(-1\\) fixes the sum \\(x+y\\).  Consequently,\nto cover a point \\((a,b)\\) that does **not** lie on \\(S_1\\cup S_2\\), at least one\nbad line must have the appropriate fixed value (its \\(x\\), its \\(y\\) or its\n\\(x+y\\)).\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) distinct columns \\(\\{x=1\\},\\dots ,\\{x=n\\}\\) intersected with\n\\(T_n\\).  Each column contains at least one point of \\(T_n\\) that is not on\n\\(S_1\\cup S_2\\): a sunny line is never vertical, so it can intersect a given\ncolumn in at most one point, leaving at least one uncovered point in that column.\nHence every column requires a distinct vertical bad line to be covered.\n\nThus we would need at least \\(n\\) vertical bad lines, but only \\(n-2\\) bad lines\nare available in total, a contradiction.  The same argument works if we look\ninstead at rows (fixed \\(y\\)) or anti\u2011diagonals (fixed \\(x+y\\)).  Therefore a\nconfiguration with exactly two sunny lines cannot exist for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Reduction to the triangle \\(T_k\\)\n\nLet \