            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to be covered.  \n\\(|T_{n}|=1+2+\\dots +n=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering\ncondition and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nLet \\(\\mathcal H\\subset\\mathcal L\\) be the subset of horizontal lines\n(\\(y=c\\)).  Because a horizontal line can cover only points whose second\ncoordinate equals its constant, the number of distinct rows that are covered by\n\\(\\mathcal H\\) is at most \\(|\\mathcal H|\\le n-k\\).  Consequently at least  \n\n\\[\nr:=n-(n-k)=k\n\\]\n\ndifferent rows are **not** covered by any horizontal line.  \nDenote these rows by  \n\n\\[\nb_{1}<b_{2}<\\dots <b_{k},\\qquad b_{i}=n-k+i .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nRow \\(b_{i}\\) contains exactly \\(n+1-b_{i}=k+1-i\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\); hence\nthe total number of points that lie in the uncovered rows equals  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{k}(k+1-i)=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nA sunny line is not horizontal, therefore it meets each row in at most one\npoint.  With \\(k\\) sunny lines we can cover at most \\(k\\) points in each of the\n\\(k\\) uncovered rows, i.e. at most \\(k^{2}\\) points in total.  From (2) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\le k^{2}\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n*Horizontal lines.*  \nTake the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n-k .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(T_{n}\\) whose second coordinate \\(b\\) satisfies \\(1\\le b\\le\nn-k\\) are covered.\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nIt remains to cover the points whose second coordinate belongs to the set  \n\n\\[\n\\{n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_{i}:\\; y=\\frac{i}{k+1-i}\\,x+\\bigl(n-k+i\\bigr).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\dfrac{i}{k+1-i}\\) is a positive rational number different from\n\\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each \\(L_{i}\\) is sunny, and the slopes are pairwise\ndifferent, so the lines are distinct.\n\n*Verification that the \\(L_{i}\\) cover the remaining points.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) satisfy (5).  Write \\(b=n-k+j\\) with\n\\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\); then the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes\n\\(1\\le a\\le k+1-j\\).  Set  \n\n\\[\ni=j-a+1 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(1\\le a\\le k+1-j\\), the integer \\(i\\) defined by (7) indeed lies in\n\\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).  Substituting this \\(i\\) into (6) we obtain\n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{j-a+1}{k+1-(j-a+1)}\\,x+n-k+j-a+1 .\n\\]\n\nEvaluating at \\(x=a\\) gives\n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{j-a+1}{k+1-j+a-1}\\,a+n-k+j-a+1\n   =n-k+j=b .\n\\]\n\nHence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_{i}\\) with \\(i\\) given by (7).  Consequently\nevery point of the set (5) belongs to **exactly one** of the lines\n\\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\).\n\nThus the family consisting of the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines (4) together with the\n\\(k\\) sunny lines (6) satisfies the covering requirement and contains exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Completion of the proof  \n\nWe have shown  \n\n* any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\) (Section 1);\n* for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a configuration with exactly\n  \\(k\\) sunny lines exists (Section 2).\n\nTherefore the set of all possible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\,}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**The problem is solved.**\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
