            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **non‑sunny** if it is parallel to one of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H:\\;y=c ,\\qquad \n\\mathcal V:\\;x=c ,\\qquad \n\\mathcal D:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nAll other lines are **sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering\ncondition and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nLet  \n\n* \\(\\mathcal H'\\subset\\mathcal L\\) be the set of horizontal lines,\n* \\(\\mathcal V'\\subset\\mathcal L\\) the set of vertical lines,\n* \\(\\mathcal D'\\subset\\mathcal L\\) the set of diagonal lines (\\(x+y=c\\)).\n\nBecause \\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\) and \\(|\\mathcal H'|+|\\mathcal V'|+|\\mathcal D'|\\le n\\),\nwe have  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal H'|\\le n-k,\\qquad |\\mathcal V'|\\le n-k,\\qquad |\\mathcal D'|\\le n-k .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nConsequently at least \\(k\\) rows, at least \\(k\\) columns and at least \\(k\\)\ndiagonals are **uncovered** by any line of the corresponding family.\nDenote the uncovered rows by  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{b_1<b_2<\\dots <b_k\\},\\qquad \nb_i=n-k+i .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n(The choice (2) is the worst possible situation for us: the uncovered rows are\nthe highest \\(k\\) rows; any other choice leaves at least as many points to be\ncovered.)\n\nA point of \\(T_n\\) that lies in an uncovered row and an uncovered column must\nsatisfy  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a\\le k+1-i,\\qquad b=n-k+i\\;(i=1,\\dots ,k),\n\\]\n\nhence the set of such points is exactly  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le k+1\\},\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nwhich contains  \n\n\\[\n|T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\npoints.\n\nA sunny line is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions; therefore\nit meets each uncovered row in **at most one** point.  Hence a single sunny line\ncontains at most \\(k\\) points of the set \\(T_k\\).  With \\(k\\) sunny lines we can\ncover at most \\(k\\cdot k=k^{2}\\) points of \\(T_k\\).\n\nSince every point of \\(T_k\\) must be covered, (4) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\le k^{2}\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus any feasible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n**Horizontal lines.**  \nTake the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n-k .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose second coordinate \\(b\\) satisfies\n\\(1\\le b\\le n-k\\).\n\n**Sunny lines for the remaining triangle.**  \nThe points that are still uncovered are precisely the set \\(T_k\\) described in\n(3) after the translation \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a,\\;b+n-k)\\); i.e. they form a right‑\nangled integer triangle of side‑length \\(k\\) whose rows are  \n\n\\[\nb=n-k+1,\\;n-k+2,\\dots ,n .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{k+1-i}\\,x+\\bigl(n-k+i\\bigr).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\dfrac{i}{k+1-i}\\) is a positive rational number different from\n\\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently every \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and the slopes are pairwise\ndifferent, so the lines are distinct.\n\n*Verification.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) be a point not covered by the horizontal lines.  Then\n\\(b=n-k+j\\) for a unique \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) and, because of the condition\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(1\\le a\\le k+1-j\\).  Set  \n\n\\[\ni=j-a+1\\qquad (1\\le i\\le k).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting this value of \\(i\\) into (8) gives  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{j-a+1}{k+1-(j-a+1)}\\,x+(n-k+j-a+1).\n\\]\n\nEvaluating at \\(x=a\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{j-a+1}{k+1-j+a-1}\\,a+n-k+j-a+1\n   =n-k+j=b .\n\\]\n\nThus \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_i\\) with \\(i\\) given by (9).  Because the\nchoice of \\(i\\) is forced by the coordinates, each point of the triangle\nbelongs to **exactly one** of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).\n\nConsequently the family consisting of the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines (6) together\nwith the \\(k\\) sunny lines (8) covers **all** points of \\(T_n\\), and exactly\n\\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Completion of the proof  \n\nWe have shown  \n\n* any configuration satisfying the requirements must have\n  \\(k\\le n-2\\) (Section 1);\n* for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) an explicit configuration\n  exists (Section 2).\n\nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2 } .\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
