olumns (\\(a\\ge n-2k+1\\)) every remaining point satisfies \\(y-x\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) and therefore lies on exactly one of the sunny lines \\(L_i\\).  \n   The columns \\(a\\le n-2k\\) are completely covered by the vertical lines \\(V_a\\).  \n\n   Counting the lines gives \\(1+k+(n-2k)=n\\); exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \n\nSince the upper bound and the explicit construction coincide, the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely the interval stated above. \u220e\n\n\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the two conditions of the problem and that exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \nLet the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines be denoted by \\(\\mathcal N\\).\n\nBecause a non\u2011sunny line can be horizontal, vertical or have slope \\(-1\\), we may replace each non\u2011sunny line by a vertical line without decreasing the set of points it covers: a vertical line \\(x=a\\) contains **all** points of column \\(a\\), while any horizontal or \\(-1\\)\u2011slope line meets a column in at most one point.  \nThus we may assume that the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines are the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThese vertical lines cover exactly the points of the first \\(n-k\\) columns of the triangle.  \nThe uncovered points are those whose first coordinate satisfies  \n\n\\[\na\\in\\{n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed \\(a\\) the column contains the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\); hence the set of uncovered points is\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal U_k=\\bigl\\{(a,b)\\mid n-k+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(\\mathcal U_k\\) is\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal U_k|\n   =\\sum_{a=n-k+1}^{n}(n+1-a)\n   =\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus after using the \\(n-k\\) vertical (non\u2011sunny) lines we must cover the \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points of \\(\\mathcal U_k\\) with the remaining \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nA sunny line is never vertical; consequently it meets each column in **at most one** point.  \nSince \\(\\mathcal U_k\\) lies in exactly \\(k\\) columns (those listed in (3)), the total number of points that the \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover is at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\cdot k = k^{2}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nTherefore we must have  \n\n\\[\nk^{2}\\;\\ge\\;|\\mathcal U_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2,\n\\qquad\\text{i.e.}\\qquad\n2k\\ge k+1.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nInequality (7) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is an integer,  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThus no configuration can contain more than \\(\\displaystyle\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\quad\\text{with}\\quad 0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nWe exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, covering all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  A non\u2011sunny diagonal\n\n\\[\nD:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(D\\) has slope \\(-1\\); it is therefore non\u2011sunny and contains every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  The sunny lines\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y-x=i .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nAll the lines \\(L_i\\) have slope \\(1