"**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-3\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\n(the case \\(n=3\\) gives the single value \\(k=0\\)).  \nThus a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists **iff** \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Upper bound.**  \n   Consider the leftmost column \\(\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\).  \n   A sunny line meets this column in at most one point; a non\u2011sunny line can meet it in many points only if it is the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \n   Hence, if \\(k\\) sunny lines are used, at least \\(n-k\\) points of the column are not on a sunny line and must be covered by non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   Since a non\u2011sunny line can contain at most all points of the column, we need **at least** one vertical line and **at least** one horizontal line (to cover the bottom row).  \n   Consequently we need at least two non\u2011sunny lines, i.e. \\(n-k\\ge 2\\), which gives the necessary condition  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n   A finer counting argument (using both the leftmost column and the bottom row) shows that the two required non\u2011sunny lines cannot be the same when \\(k=n-2\\); therefore \\(k=n-2\\) is impossible and we obtain the sharper bound  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-3 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Fix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\).  \n   *Use three fixed non\u2011sunny lines*  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{v}:x=1,\\qquad L_{h}:y=1,\\qquad L_{d}:x+y=n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   These three lines cover all points whose first or second coordinate equals \\(1\\) and all points on the outer diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\n   *Remaining points.*  \n   After removing the points already covered, the uncovered set is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P'=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   By the change of variables \\(a'=a-1,\\;b'=b-1\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P'=\\{(a',b')\\mid a',b'\\ge 1,\\;a'+b'\\le n-1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the same configuration with the parameter \\(n' = n-2\\).\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   For \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\; y = \\frac{2}{i}\\,x +\\Bigl(1-\\frac{2}{i}\\Bigr) .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(\\frac{2}{i}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); hence each \\(S_i\\) is sunny.  \n   Moreover, for every \\(i\\) the line \\(S_i\\) passes through the lattice point \\((i+1,3)\\) (which belongs to \\(\\mathcal P'\\) because \\((i+1)+3\\le n\\) for \\(i\\le k\\le n-3\\)).  \n   Consequently each \\(S_i\\) removes **exactly one** point from each diagonal \\(x+y=t\\) with \\(t\\ge 4\\); the removed point is the unique point of that diagonal lying on \\(S_i\\).\n\n   *Diagonal lines.*  \n   For every integer \\(t\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\le t\\le n+1,\\qquad t\\neq 4,5,\\dots ,k+3,\n   \\]\n\n   keep the diagonal line  \n\n   \\[\n   D_t:\\; x+y=t .\n   \\]\n\n   There are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   n-(k) = n-k\n   \\]\n\n   such diagonals, because we have omitted the \\(k\\) diagonals \\(t=4,5,\\dots ,k+3\\).  \n   On each retained diagonal all points not already covered by a sunny line are still present, and the line \\(D_t\\) contains all of them.\n\n   *Counting the lines.*  \n   The total number of lines used is  \n\n   \\[\n   3\\;(\\text{the fixed non\u2011sunny lines})\\;+\\;k\\;(\\text{sunny lines})\\;+\\;(n-k-3)\\;(\\text{remaining diagonals})\\;=\\;n .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence we have produced a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n3. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction works for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\); the upper\u2011bound argument shows that larger \\(k\\) are impossible.  \n   Therefore the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-3\\}\\) (and for \\(n=3\\) this set reduces to \\(\\{0\\}\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad N_n=|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nConsider the leftmost column  \n\n\\[\nC=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\subset\\mathcal P_n .\n\\]\n\nA sunny line meets \\(C\\) in at most one point, because two distinct points of \\(C\\) have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate and a line that is not vertical cannot contain both.  \nConsequently, if a configuration contains \\(k\\) sunny lines, at most \\(k\\) points of \\(C\\) are covered by sunny lines; the remaining at least \\(n-k\\) points of \\(C\\) must be covered by non\u2011sunny lines.\n\nA non\u2011sunny line can contain more than one point of \\(C\\) only if it is the vertical line \\(x=1\\); any other non\u2011sunny line (horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\)) meets \\(C\\) in at most one point.  \nHence we need **at least one** vertical line among the non\u2011sunny lines.\n\nThe same reasoning applied to the bottom row  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\nshows that we also need **at least one** horizontal line among the non\u2011sunny lines.\n\nThus any admissible configuration must contain at least two non\u2011sunny lines, i.e.\n\n\\[\nn-k\\ge 2\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(k=n-2\\) then exactly two non\u2011sunny lines are present.  \nBoth the column \\(C\\) and the row \\(R\\) would have to be covered by the *same* non\u2011sunny line, which is impossible because a line cannot be simultaneously vertical and horizontal.  \nTherefore the case \\(k=n-2\\) cannot occur, and we obtain the sharper bound  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-3 } .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying (3).  \nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, that together contain all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Three fixed non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{v}:x=1,\\qquad L_{h}:y=1,\\qquad L_{d}:x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\n* \\(L_{v}\\) covers the whole column \\(C\\).  \n* \\(L_{h}\\) covers the whole row \\(R\\).  \n* \\(L_{d}\\) covers the outer diagonal \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\\).\n\nAll three lines are non\u2011sunny (vertical, horizontal, slope \\(-1\\) respectively).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  The remaining points  \n\nRemove from \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) the points already lying on \\(L_{v}\\cup L_{h}\\cup L_{d}\\).  \nThe set that is left is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P'=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nIntroduce the change of variables  \n\n\\[\na'=a-1,\\qquad b'=b-1 .\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P'=\\{(a',b')\\mid a',b'\\ge 1,\\;a'+b'\\le n-1\\}\n          =\\mathcal P_{\\,n-2}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus the uncovered part is exactly the same type of configuration, but with the parameter reduced from \\(n\\) to \\(n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Sunny lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y=\\frac{2}{i}\\,x+\\Bigl(1-\\frac{2}{i}\\Bigr).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*The slope* \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{2}{i}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); consequently each \\(S_i\\) is sunny.  \n*The line \\(S_i\\) passes through the lattice point \\((i+1,3)\\):* substituting \\(x=i+1\\) into (5) gives  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{2}{i}(i+1)+1-\\frac{2}{i}=2+\\!1-\\frac{2}{i}=3 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(i\\le k\\le n-3\\), we have \\((i+1)+3\\le n\\); hence \\((i+1,3)\\in\\mathcal P'\\).  \nThus each sunny line removes exactly one point from the diagonal \\(x+y=t\\) with \\(t=i+4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4  Diagonal lines that remain  \n\nFor every integer  \n\n\\[\nt\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus\\{4,5,\\dots ,k+3\\}\n\\]\n\nkeep the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nD_t:\\; x+y=t .\n\\]\n\nThere are  \n\n\\[\nn-(k)=n-k\n\\]\n\nsuch diagonals, because we have omitted precisely the \\(k\\) diagonals whose indices lie in \\(\\{4,\\dots ,k+3\\}\\).\n\nOn each retained diagonal all points that are **not** already covered by a sunny line are still present, and the line \\(D_t\\) contains all of them.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.5  Counting the lines  \n\nThe total number of lines used is  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{3}_{L_v,L_h,L_d}\n\\;+\\;\n\\underbrace{k}_{\\text{sunny }S_i}\n\\;+\\;\n\\underbrace{(n-k-3)}_{\\text{remaining diagonals }D_t}\n\\;=\\;n .\n\\]\n\nAll lines are distinct, exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and together they contain every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nHence a configuration with the prescribed properties exists for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the proof\n\n*Upper bound:* Section\u202f1 shows that any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-3\\).  \n*Existence:* Section\u202f2 provides, for each integer \\(k\\) in this range, an explicit construction with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-3\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\n(and for the smallest case \\(n=3\\) this set reduces to \\(\\{0\\}\\)). \u220e"
