            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nConsider the leftmost column  \n\n\\[\nC=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\subset\\mathcal P_n .\n\\]\n\nA sunny line is not vertical, hence it can contain at most one point of \\(C\\).  \nConsequently, if a configuration contains \\(k\\) sunny lines, at most \\(k\\) points of \\(C\\) are covered by sunny lines; the remaining at least \\(n-k\\) points of \\(C\\) must be covered by non‑sunny lines.\n\nA non‑sunny line can contain more than one point of \\(C\\) only if it is the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \nThus **the line \\(x=1\\) must be present** among the non‑sunny lines.\n\nThe same reasoning applied to the bottom row  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\nshows that the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) must also be present.\n\nFinally, the set of points with \\(a+b=n+1\\) forms the diagonal  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich has slope \\(-1\\).  Since a sunny line is forbidden to have slope \\(-1\\), \\(\\Delta\\) must be covered by a line of slope \\(-1\\); we may take the line \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\nHence **any admissible configuration contains at least three non‑sunny lines**.  \nIf the total number of lines is \\(n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, we have  \n\n\\[\nn-k\\ge 3\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\le n-3 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(k>n-3\\) is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\)\n\nFix such a \\(k\\).  We shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, covering all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Three fixed non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{v}:x=1,\\qquad L_{h}:y=1,\\qquad L_{d}:x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThese three lines are respectively vertical, horizontal and of slope \\(-1\\); they are non‑sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Points still uncovered  \n\nRemove from \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) the points lying on the lines in (2).  \nThe remaining set is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P'=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIntroduce the change of variables  \n\n\\[\na'=a-1,\\qquad b'=b-1 .\n\\]\n\nThen (3) becomes  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P'=\\{(a',b')\\mid a',b'\\ge 1,\\;a'+b'\\le n-1\\}\n          =\\mathcal P_{\\,n-2}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus the uncovered points form exactly the same configuration with the parameter reduced from \\(n\\) to \\(n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Sunny lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y=\\frac{2}{i}\\,x+\\Bigl(1-\\frac{2}{i}\\Bigr).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n* The slope \\(\\frac{2}{i}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(S_i\\) is sunny.  \n* Substituting \\(x=i+1\\) into (5) gives \\(y=3\\); hence \\(S_i\\) passes through the lattice point \\((i+1,3)\\).  \n* Because \\(i\\le k\\le n-3\\), we have \\((i+1)+3\\le n\\); consequently \\((i+1,3)\\in\\mathcal P'\\).  \n\nThus \\(S_i\\) covers exactly one lattice point of the diagonal \\(x+y=i+4\\).\n\nThe lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) are distinct because their slopes \\(\\frac{2}{i}\\) are distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4  Remaining diagonal lines  \n\nThe diagonals of \\(\\mathcal P'\\) are the lines  \n\n\\[\nx+y=t\\qquad\\text{with }4\\le t\\le n .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFor the \\(k\\) values \\(t=4,5,\\dots ,k+3\\) the diagonal already contains the point covered by the sunny line \\(S_{t-4}\\); we therefore **omit** those diagonals.\n\nFor every other integer  \n\n\\[\nt\\in\\{k+4,k+5,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nkeep the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nD_t:\\; x+y=t .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nOn each such diagonal no lattice point has been covered by a sunny line (the only lattice points that a sunny line meets are the points \\((i+1,3)\\) with \\(i\\le k\\), which lie on the omitted diagonals).  Hence \\(D_t\\) contains all remaining points of that diagonal.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.5  Counting the lines  \n\n*Non‑sunny lines*  \n\n* the three fixed lines \\(L_v, L_h, L_d\\);  \n* the diagonals \\(D_t\\) for \\(t=k+4,\\dots ,n\\).  \n  Their number is \\(n-(k+3)=n-k-3\\).\n\nThus the total number of non‑sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n3+(n-k-3)=n-k .\n\\]\n\n*Sunny lines* – exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\).\n\nConsequently the whole configuration consists of  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)\\;+\\;k\\;=\\;n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the proof\n\nSection 1 shows that any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-3\\).  \nSection 2 constructs, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\), a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which such a configuration exists is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-3\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\n(and for \\(n=3\\) this set reduces to \\(\\{0\\}\\)). ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
