emma 1 (Maximum number of points of \\(S_n\\) on a sunny line)\n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line.  Then  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap S_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(L\\) in reduced rational form  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{p}{q}x+c ,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\; \\gcd(p,q)=1,\n\\]\n\nwith \\(pq\\neq0\\) (otherwise \\(L\\) would be horizontal or vertical) and\n\\(p\\neq -q\\) (otherwise \\(L\\) would be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\nIf \\((a,b)\\) and \\((a',b')\\) are two distinct lattice points of \\(L\\), then\n\\((a'-a,b'-b)=(q,p)\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n(a'+b')-(a+b)=p+q .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p\\neq -q\\) we have \\(|p+q|\\ge 2\\).  Inside the strip\n\\(2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\) the integer value of \\(a+b\\) can change at most by steps of\nsize \\(\\ge2\\); consequently at most  \n\n\\[\n1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{(n+1)-2}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\le 1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\ndifferent points of \\(S_n\\) can lie on \\(L\\).  ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (All‑sunny configuration is impossible for \\(n\\ge4\\))\n\nIf all \\(n\\) lines are sunny, then \\(n\\le3\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume the contrary, i.e. \\(n\\ge4\\) and all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\nBy Lemma 1 each line contains at most \\(\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\) points of\n\\(S_n\\); therefore\n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge4\\) one has \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le\\frac{n}{2}\\),\nand the right‑hand side of (6) is at most \\(n\\cdot\\frac{n}{2}= \\frac{n^{2}}2\\),\nwhich is **strictly smaller** than \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).  This contradiction shows\nthat an all‑sunny family cannot exist when \\(n\\ge4\\).  ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Existence of a covering with exactly \\(k\\) sunny 