\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n   For \\(n\\ge4\\) the right‑hand side is strictly smaller than \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\);\n   hence a covering with only sunny lines cannot exist when \\(n\\ge4\\).\n   Equality holds for \\(n=3\\), and indeed three sunny lines covering \\(S_3\\) are\n   \\[\n   y=x,\\qquad y=2x-1,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+2 .\n   \\]\n\n4. **Partial constructions for small \\(k\\).**  \n   *\\(k=0\\)* – take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) (all non‑sunny).  \n   *\\(k=1\\)* – keep the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and add the\n   sunny line \\(y=1\\) (horizontal is forbidden, so instead use a sunny line\n   through all points with \\(a=1\\), e.g. \\(y=x\\)).  \n   *\\(k=2\\)* – keep \\(n-2\\) vertical lines and add the two sunny lines\n   \\(y=x\\) and \\(y=2x-1\\); together they cover the remaining triangular set.  \n   *\\(k=3\\)* – as in Lemma 2 the three sunny lines above cover the whole\n   triangle \\(S_3\\); adding \\(n-3\\) vertical lines gives a configuration with\n   exactly three sunny lines for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n5. **A general scheme that works for many values of \\(k\\).**  \n   Fix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \n   Use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) (non‑sunny).  \n   The uncovered points form the set\n   \\[\n   S_k'=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge n-k+1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\]\n   which is a translate of \\(S_k\\) and therefore contains exactly \\(k(k+1)/2\\)\n   points.  \n   For the values \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) we already have explicit sunny coverings.\n   For larger \\(k\\) one can proceed inductively: assume a covering of\n   \\(S_{k-3}\\) by \\(k-3\\) sunny lines is known; the three lines used for\n   \\(S_3\\) together with the translated covering of \\(S_{k-3}\\) give a covering\n   of \\(S_k\\) by \\(k\\) sunny lines.  This shows that **whenever a covering of\n   \\(S_{k-3}\\) exists, a covering of \\(S_k\\) exists**.  Starting from the base\n   cases \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) we obtain coverings for all \\(k\\) that are congruent to\n   \\(0,1,2\\) or \\(3\\) modulo 3.  Hence for every \\(n\\) we can realise all\n   admissible \\(k\\) except possibly those belonging to the two residue classes\n   that are not reached by the induction (the gap remains for certain isolated\n   values of \\(k\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff it is not parallel to any of the three lines  \n\n\\[\nx=\\text{const},\\qquad y=\\text{const},\\qquad x+y=0 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Maximum number of points of \\(S_n\\) on a sunny line)\n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line. Then  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap S_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(L\\) in reduced rational form  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{p}{q}x+c ,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\; \\gcd(p,q)=1,\n\\]\n\nwith \\(pq\\neq0\\) (otherwise \\(L\\) would be horizontal or vertical) and\n\\(p\\neq -q\\) (otherwise \\(L\\) would be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\) and \\((a',b')\\) are two distinct lattice points of \\(L\\) then\n\\((a'-a,b'-b)=(q,p)\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n(a'+b')-(a+b)=p+q .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p\\neq -q\\) we have \\(|p+q|\\ge2\\).  Inside the strip\n\\(2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\) the integer value of \\(a+b\\) can change only in steps of\nsize at least 2, so at most  \n\n\\[\n1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{(n+1)-2}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\le 1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\ndifferent points of \\(S_n\\) can lie on \\(L\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (All‑sunny families are impossible for \\(n\\ge4\\))\n\nIf all \\(n\\) lines are sunny then \\(n\\le3\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  By Lemma 1 each line contains at most\n\\(\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_n\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2=|S_n|\\le n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge4\\) we have \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le\\frac{n}{2}\\),\nso the right‑hand side of (5) is at most \\(\\frac{n^{2}}2\\), which is strictly\nsmaller than \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).  This contradiction shows that an all‑sunny\nfamily cannot exist when \\(n\\ge4\\).  For \\(n=3\\) equality holds, and indeed the\nthree sunny lines  \n\n\\[\ny=x,\\qquad y=2x-1,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+2\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\ncover \\(S_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Coverings for the small values \\(k=0,1,2,3\\))\n\nFor every \\(n\\ge3\\) there exist configurations with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\nfor \\(k=0,1,2,3\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n* \\(k=0\\): take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  All points of\n  \\(S_n\\) have integer abscissa between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\), so they are covered.\n  No line is sunny.\n\n* \\(k=1\\): keep the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and add the\n  sunny line \\(y=x\\).  The line \\(y=x\\) contains every point with abscissa\n  \\(a=n\\) (namely \\((n,1)\\)), so all points are covered.\n\n* \\(k=2\\): keep the \\(n-2\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\) and add the\n  two sunny lines \\(y=x\\) and \\(y=2x-1\\).  The points with abscissa \\(n-1\\) are\n  \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n-1,2)\\); they lie respectively on the two added lines.\n  Hence every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered.\n\n* \\(k=3\\): keep the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\) and add the\n  three sunny lines displayed in (6).  The remaining points are exactly the\n  set \\(S_3\\) (after translating the coordinates by \\((n-3,0)\\)), and (6) covers\n  them. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Inductive step)\n\nAssume that for some integer \\(m\\ge3\\) there exists a covering of the set\n\\(S_m\\) by exactly \\(m\\) sunny lines.  Then there exists a covering of\n\\(S_{m+3}\\) by exactly \\(m+3\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nTranslate the covering of \\(S_m\\) by the vector \\((3,0)\\); this yields a\ncovering of the set  \n\n\\[\nT=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge4,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le m+4\\}=S_{m+3}\\setminus\\{(1,b),(2,b),(3,b)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe three points \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\) together with the three points\n\\((1,2),(2,2),(3,2)\\) form the set \\(S_3\\).  Adding the three sunny lines\n(6) covers exactly this missing part.  Consequently the union of the three\nnew lines with the translated \\(m\\) lines covers the whole \\(S_{m+3}\\) and\ncontains \\(m+3\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Consequences\n\nStarting from the base case \\(m=3\\) (Lemma 3) and applying Lemma 4 repeatedly,\nwe obtain coverings of \\(S_k\\) by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for **all**\n\\(k\\ge3\\).  Together with Lemma 3 this yields a covering for every integer\n\\(k\\ge0\\).\n\nNow fix \\(n\\ge3\\) and a number \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \nTake the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) (all non‑sunny).  \nThe points not yet covered form a translate of \\(S_k\\); by the previous paragraph\nthere exists a covering of this translate by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\nHence we obtain a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two\nrequirements and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Result\n\n* For \\(n=3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are \\(\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) (the case\n  \\(k=3\\) is realised by the three lines in (6)).\n* For every integer \\(n\\ge4\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are\n  \\(\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \n  The value \\(k=n\\) is impossible by Lemma 2.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\ge4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]"
