rizontal line can cover at most one additional diagonal point, so at least \\(n-3\\) further non‑sunny lines would be required, exceeding the allowed number. Hence a slope \\(-1\\) line is indeed necessary.\n\n#### 2.4 Upper bound conclusion  \n\n> “If the total number of lines is \\(n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, then \\(n-k\\ge 3\\) … \\(k\\le n-3\\).”\n\n*Correct* provided the diagonal argument is justified.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Existence for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\)\n\n#### 3.1 Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\n> “\\(\\mathcal P_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\). The three lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=4\\) … give \\(k=0=n-3\\).”\n\n**Critical Error.**  \nFor \\(n=3\\) the condition \\(a+b\\le 4\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) yields the six points  \n\\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \nThe solution lists only three of them, omitting \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\). Consequently the three lines cited do **not** cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_3\\). The base case is false, and the inductive construction that follows has no valid starting point. This error invalidates the existence proof.\n\n#### 3.2 Inductive step (statement of hypothesis)\n\n> “Assume the statement true for some integer \\(n-2\\ge 3\\).”\n\nBecause the base case is false, the induction cannot be anchored; the hypothesis is unsupported.\n\n#### 3.3 Reduction to \\(\\mathcal P_{\\,n-2}\\)\n\n> “Remove the points lying on \\(L_v\\cup L_h\\cup L_d\\). The remaining set is \\(\\mathcal P'=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le n\\}\\). With the change of variables … \\(\\mathcal P'=\\mathcal P_{\\,n-2}\\).”\n\n*Correct* transformation, assuming the three fixed lines indeed cover exactly the points removed.\n\n#### 3.4 Case \\(k\\le n-5\\)\n\n> “Apply the induction hypothesis … obtain a family \\(\\mathcal L'\\) of \\(n-2\\) distinct lines … The union \\(\\mathcal L = \\{L_v,L_h,L_d\\}\\cup\\mathcal L'\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines …”\n\n*Correct* **provided** the induction hypothesis hol