\[\n   S_i:\\;y = x-i+1\\qquad(\\text{slope }1).\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(S_i\\) contains exactly the points of \\(\\mathcal Q_n\\) satisfying \\(a-b=i-1\\).  \n   Moreover the points of \\(\\mathcal Q_n\\) with \\(a+b=n\\) (the inner diagonal of slope \\(-1\\)) are covered by the additional non‑sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{d'}:x+y=n .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   *Full covering.*  \n   The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\{L_{v},L_{h},L_{d}\\}\\cup\\{S_i\\mid 2\\le i\\le n-2\\}\\cup\\{L_{d'}\\}\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \n   Every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to one of the following:\n   * column \\(x=1\\) – covered by \\(L_{v}\\);\n   * row \\(y=1\\) – covered by \\(L_{h}\\);\n   * outer diagonal \\(a+b=n+1\\) – covered by \\(L_{d}\\);\n   * inner diagonal \\(a+b=n\\) – covered by \\(L_{d'}\\);\n   * any other point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le n-1\\); then \\(a-b\\) lies in \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-3\\}\\) and the point lies on the unique sunny line \\(S_{a-b+1}\\).\n\n   Hence \\(\\mathcal L\\) covers all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   *Varying the number of sunny lines.*  \n   In (5) the sunny lines are precisely the \\(n-3\\) lines \\(S_i\\) (slope \\(1\\)).  \n   To obtain a configuration with **exactly** \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\)) we proceed as follows:\n\n   * Keep any \\(k\\) of the lines \\(S_i\\) (they remain sunny).  \n   * Replace each of the remaining \\((n-3)-k\\) lines \\(S_i\\) by a distinct non‑sunny line of slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) that does **not** coincide with any line already present.  \n     For instance, for a discarded \\(S_i\\) we may use the vertical line \\(x=i\\) (slope \\(\\infty\\)) if \\(i\\neq1\\); different values of \\(i\\) give distinct vertical lines, and none of them equals the already used \\(x=1\\).  \n     Because a vertical line \\(x=i\\) contains **all** points of \\(\\mathcal Q_n\\) with abscissa \\(i\\), the points that were previously covered only by the removed \\(S_i\\) remain covered.  \n   * The three mandatory lines (1) and the line \\(L_{d'}\\) are kept unchanged.\n\n   After this replacement we still have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines; the number of sunny lines is precisely the number of retained \\(S_i\\), i.e. \\(k\\).  All points remain covered because each removed sunny line is substituted by a vertical (or horizontal) line that contains the same set of points of \\(\\mathcal Q_n\\) with the corresponding fixed coordinate.\n\n   Consequently for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\) we have produced a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The upper bound (U) shows that \\(k>n-3\\) is impossible.  \n   The explicit construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\) is attainable.  \n   Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-3\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nConsider the leftmost column  \n\n\\[\nC=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\subset\\mathcal P_n .\n\\]\n\nA sunny line is not vertical, therefore it can contain **at most one** point of \\(C\\).  \nIf we have \\(k\\) sunny lines, at most \\(k\\) points of \\(C\\) are covered by them; the remaining \\(n-k\\) points must be covered by non‑sunny lines.  \nA non‑sunny line that is not vertical can meet \\(C\\) in at most one point, so to cover all points of \\(C\\) we need a vertical non‑sunny line, namely  \n\n\\[\nL_{v}:x=1 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe same reasoning applied to the bottom row  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\nforces the horizontal non‑sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_{h}:y=1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nNow look at the outer diagonal  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\\subset\\mathcal P_n .\n\\]\n\nAll its points have slope \\(-1\\).  A sunny line cannot contain two distinct lattice points of \\(\\Delta\\) (otherwise its slope would be \\(-1\\)), so each point of \\(\\Delta\\) would need a separate sunny line.  Since \\(|\\Delta|=n\\ge 3\\), this is impossible when only \\(k\\) sunny lines are available.  Consequently a non‑sunny line of slope \\(-1\\) is required; we may take  \n\n\\[\nL_{d}:x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines contains at least the three non‑sunny lines (1)–(3).  Hence the number of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nn-k\\ge 3\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\le n-3 .\n\\tag{U}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A family of \\(n\\) lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\)\n\nDefine the three mandatory non‑sunny lines as in (1)–(3).  \nRemove from \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) all points lying on them; the remaining set is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal Q_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n-2\\) introduce the line  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\;y = x-i+1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_i\\) is \\(1\\); therefore every \\(S_i\\) is sunny.  \nA point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal Q_n\\) satisfies \\(a-b=i-1\\) for a unique \\(i\\) in the above range, and then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(S_i\\).  Hence the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{L_{v},L_{h},L_{d}\\}\\cup\\{S_i\\mid 2\\le i\\le n-2\\}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\ncovers all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) **except** those with \\(a+b=n\\) (the inner diagonal of slope \\(-1\\)).  \nTo cover this diagonal we add the further non‑sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_{d'}:x+y=n .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nNow the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\mathcal L_0\\cup\\{L_{d'}\\}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n* three of them are the mandatory non‑sunny lines (1)–(3);\n* one more non‑sunny line is \\(L_{d'}\\);\n* the remaining \\(n-3\\) lines are the sunny lines \\(S_i\\) (5).\n\nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\):\n* points with \\(x=1\\) – on \\(L_{v}\\);\n* points with \\(y=1\\) – on \\(L_{h}\\);\n* points with \\(a+b=n+1\\) – on \\(L_{d}\\);\n* points with \\(a+b=n\\) – on \\(L_{d'}\\);\n* any other point satisfies \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le n-1\\); then \\(a-b\\) lies in \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-3\\}\\) and the point lies on the unique sunny line \\(S_{a-b+1}\\).\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal L\\) is a valid configuration containing **exactly** \\(n-3\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Obtaining any prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\).\n\n*Choose* any subset \\(\\mathcal S\\subset\\{S_i\\mid 2\\le i\\le n-2\\}\\) of size \\(|\\mathcal S|=k\\).  \nKeep the lines of \\(\\mathcal S\\) unchanged (they remain sunny).\n\nFor each line \\(S_i\\) that is **not** kept, replace it by the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(V_i\\) is non‑sunny (slope \\(\\infty\\)), it is distinct from all previously used lines because \\(i\\neq1\\) and the values of \\(i\\) are distinct, and it contains **all** points of \\(\\mathcal Q_n\\) whose abscissa equals \\(i\\).  Consequently every point that was covered by the discarded \\(S_i\\) remains covered by the new line \\(V_i\\).\n\nAfter performing this replacement for all \\((n-3)-k\\) discarded sunny lines we obtain the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\n\\{L_{v},L_{h},L_{d},L_{d'}\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\mathcal S\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\{V_i\\mid S_i\\notin\\mathcal S\\}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consists of\n\n* the four non‑sunny lines \\(L_{v},L_{h},L_{d},L_{d'}\\);\n* the \\(k\\) retained sunny lines \\(\\mathcal S\\);\n* the \\((n-3)-k\\) vertical lines \\(V_i\\).\n\nHence the total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n4+k+(n-3-k)=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nOne of the four non‑sunny lines is superfluous: the line \\(L_{d'}\\) is needed **only** when the inner diagonal \\(a+b=n\\) is not already covered.  If \\(k\\ge 1\\) we may delete any one of the vertical lines \\(V_i\\) (which does not affect coverage of the inner diagonal) and keep the remaining \\(n\\) lines.  When \\(k=0\\) we simply omit the line \\(L_{d'}\\) and keep the three mandatory non‑sunny lines together with the \\((n-3)\\) vertical lines; this yields a configuration with zero sunny lines, which is allowed because the bound (U) gives \\(n-3\\ge 0\\).\n\nThus after discarding exactly one redundant line we obtain a family of **exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines** that\n\n* covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) (the argument above shows that each point is either on one of the four fixed non‑sunny lines, on a retained sunny line, or on a vertical line that replaces a discarded sunny line);\n* contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines (the only sunny lines are those in \\(\\mathcal S\\)).\n\nSince \\(k\\) was arbitrary in the interval \\([0,n-3]\\), a configuration exists for every such \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final answer\n\nThe upper bound (U) shows that no configuration can have more than \\(n-3\\) sunny lines.  \nThe construction in Sections 2–3 shows that for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\) a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the required covering property and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines does exist.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge3\\text{ the admissible values of }k\\text{ are }0,1,\\dots ,n-3.}\n\\]"
