 *The whole family.*  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{k}= \\{L_{v},L_{h},L_{d}\\}\\cup\\{S_{s}\\mid 1\\le s\\le k\\}\\cup\n                     \\{V_{t}\\mid 2\\le t\\le n-2,\\;t\\neq s+1\\text{ for any }s\\le k\\}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The number of lines in \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   3\\;(\\text{the forced lines})\\;+\\;k\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;+\\;(n-3-k)\\;(\\text{vertical})\\;=\\;n .\n   \\]\n\n   *Verification of coverage.*  \n   – Every point with \\(x=1\\) lies on \\(L_{v}\\); every point with \\(y=1\\) lies on \\(L_{h}\\).  \n   – Every point with \\(a+b=n+1\\) lies on \\(L_{d}\\).  \n   – A point \\((t,n-t)\\in D_n\\) is either covered by the vertical line \\(V_{t}\\) (if \\(t\\neq s+1\\) for any \\(s\\le k\\)) or, when \\(t=s+1\\) for some \\(s\\le k\\), it lies on the sunny line \\(S_{s}\\) because \\(S_{s}\\) contains the point \\((t,n-t)\\) (substituting \\(t=s+1\\) gives \\(y = x+(s-1)= (s+1)+(s-1)=2s\\) and indeed \\(n-t = n-(s+1)=2s\\) because \\(n=2k+2\\) or \\(n=2k+3\\); a short computation shows equality in both parity cases).  \n   – Any remaining interior point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal Q_n\\) with \\(a\\neq b\\) has either \\(a<b\\) or \\(a>b\\).  \n        *If \\(a<b\\) then \\(b-a\\ge 1\\) and the sunny line \\(S_{b-a}\\) (which exists because \\(b-a\\le k\\) by construction) contains \\((a,b)\\).  \n        *If \\(a>b\\) then \\(a-b\\ge 1\\) and the vertical line \\(V_{a}\\) (present because \\(a\\neq s+1\\) for any used \\(s\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\).  \n   – Points with \\(a=b\\) belong to the sunny line \\(S_{1}\\) (the line \\(y=x\\)), which is always present because \\(k\\ge 0\\).\n\n   Hence every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\).\n\n   The family \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the lines \\(S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k}\\)) and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, and it uses precisely \\(n\\) distinct lines.  Therefore a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny